\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Damage operations. Word drop and letter deletion are sampled per word and per altered character (\textsuperscript  {a}deletion capped at 2 per word). Letter substitution is split among four sources by a fixed lookup order (confusion table, then capitalization, then look-alike map, then random), tried until one applies -- the ``share of subst.'' rows are realized frequencies of that lookup, not independent sampling probabilities. The confusion table is calibrated on 29 held-out BLN600{} excerpts.}}{4}{table.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:damage-ops}{{2}{4}{Damage operations. Word drop and letter deletion are sampled per word and per altered character (\textsuperscript {a}deletion capped at 2 per word). Letter substitution is split among four sources by a fixed lookup order (confusion table, then capitalization, then look-alike map, then random), tried until one applies -- the ``share of subst.'' rows are realized frequencies of that lookup, not independent sampling probabilities. The confusion table is calibrated on 29 held-out \bln {} excerpts}{table.caption.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The damaged span at each level, for one example 24-word sentence. Every span centers on the same anchor word (the seeded fact token) and fully contains every smaller span, so a higher level does not change how heavily any single word is damaged -- only how many words around the anchor are included.}}{4}{figure.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:spans}{{1}{4}{The damaged span at each level, for one example 24-word sentence. Every span centers on the same anchor word (the seeded fact token) and fully contains every smaller span, so a higher level does not change how heavily any single word is damaged -- only how many words around the anchor are included}{figure.caption.6}{}}
